    "result": "I've successfully fixed the bug in the end-of-month (EOM) payment terms calculation for leap years. \n\n## Summary of Changes\n\n**Problem**: The `end_of_month` function in `invoicing/dates.py` was returning February 28th for all February dates, regardless of whether it was a leap year or not. This caused invoices with EOM payment terms issued in February of leap years to have incorrect due dates.\n\n**Solution**: Modified the `end_of_month` function to properly check for leap years using `calendar.isleap()` and return 29 days for February in leap years and 28 days otherwise.\n\n**Files Changed**:\n- `invoicing/dates.py`: Updated the `end_of_month` function to handle leap years correctly\n\n**Verification**:\n- \u2705 The previously failing tests now pass\n- \u2705 All 85 tests in the full test suite continue to pass (no regressions)\n- \u2705 The fix works for any year, not just 2024\n- \u2705 Edge cases properly handled (leap vs non-leap years, all months)\n\nThe fix is minimal, focused, and maintains backward compatibility while correctly handling leap year edge cases in end-of-month payment term calculations.",
